  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nSatellite imagery this afternoon showcases the cold front that \nbrought our overnight showers moving through WI with a field of \ncumulus across central and northern Minnesota, with an area \nbetween the front and cumulus showcasing some wispy smoke from \nwildfires over the western Rockies. Little of this smoke is \nexpected to reach the surface but it could produce enough of a \nsky mask to knock high temperatures down by a degree or two this\nafternoon, with comfortable low to mid 70s expected. Low \ntemperatures tonight will likewise be comfortable in the low 50s\noutside of the urban heat island, with upper 50s within the \nTwin Cities. The upper level smoke is not expected to make it to\nthe surface, so you should be safe to open the windows and get \nsome fresh air into your home should you choose to do so. \nTomorrow looks to be a bit warmer than today with near 80 high \ntemperatures and mostly sunny skies, with the breeziest winds \nthis week arriving on Thursday with some frequent gusts around \n20mph out of the south ahead of our next cold front dropping \ndown later in the day on Friday.\n\nQuiet weather is expected to continue today and through Thursday \nuntil our next precipitation chances arrive Friday \nevening/night. Ensembles continue to show very different ideas \non upcoming temperatures with the GEFS wanting a push back into \nthe low 90s by Friday and into the weekend with the much cooler \nEPS peaking in the mid 80s. This also shows within the longer \nrange forecast with some sporadic storm chances arriving this \nweekend with fairly low confidence given the competing \nsolutions. The warmer GEFS would favor more instability and with\nany sort of forcing would promote some stronger storms, however\ncould also result in more capping if the lower-mid levels heat \nup more. Speaking of forcing, a broad frontal boundary will slow\nmove from north to south across Minnesota beginning Friday \nnight and lasting into early next week, with relatively weak \nforcing given the lack of a strong surface low and weak upper \nlevel reinforcement. We are certainly showcasing the \nsouthwestern retreat of the heat dome in this situation, with \nour upper level flow turning more northwesterly causing our \nupstream flow to come from the PAC NW instead of to the south, \nlimiting our moisture and also introducing a likelihood of some \nupper level smoke helping to knock down temperatures. Our \nexpectation would be to see temperatures settle closer to the \nlow 80s as we head into mid August, which would be around our \naverage high temperature for this time of year if slightly above\nnormal.",
